Network reconnaissance is the process of gathering information about systems, hosts, ports, and services on a network.

An attacker may perform reconnaissance to determine:
	*	Which hosts are online.
	*	Which ports are open.
	*	Which services are available.
	*	Which systems may be vulnerable.
	*	What services could potentially be targeted.


From a SOC perspective, reconnaissance can be an important early indicator of possible malicious activity.

Understanding a TCP SYN Scan

A TCP SYN scan is a common Nmap scanning technique.

The basic TCP process normally involves:

Client → SYN
Server → SYN/ACK
Client → ACK

This is the normal TCP three-way handshake.

With a SYN scan, the scanner sends a SYN packet and observes the response without completing the full TCP connection.

For example:

Kali
192.168.56.109
       |
       | TCP SYN
       v
Ubuntu
192.168.56.105

The scanner can use the response to determine whether a port appears open, closed, or filtered.

This makes SYN scanning useful for reconnaissance.

Suricata Configuration

One of the important parts of the project was configuring Suricata to monitor the correct network interface.

The monitoring interface was:

enp0s8

The host-only network was:

192.168.56.0/24

Suricata needed to monitor the interface carrying the traffic between Kali and Ubuntu.

This taught me an important practical lesson:

	An IDS cannot detect traffic it is not monitoring.

Correctly identifying the network interface is therefore an important part of IDS deployment.

Suricata Rule

I created a custom Suricata detection rule for TCP SYN scanning.

The rule was:

alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"TCP SYN Scan Detected"; flags S; sid:1000001;)

Breaking Down the Rule

alert

Tells Suricata to generate an alert when the rule matches.

tcp

The rule applies to TCP traffic.

any any

The source IP and source port can be any value.

->

Represents the direction of the traffic.

any any

The destination IP and destination port can also be any value.

msg:"TCP SYN Scan Detected"

This is the message displayed when the rule triggers.

flags S

This checks for the TCP SYN flag.

The SYN flag is used when initiating TCP communication.

sid:1000001

This is the unique Suricata Signature ID for the custom rule.

Rule Location

The custom rule was stored in:

/etc/suricata/rules/suricata.rules

The Suricata configuration referenced the rules directory:

/etc/suricata/rules

This helped me understand that Suricata depends on correctly configured rule paths and filenames.

Suricata Configuration Troubleshooting

One of the most valuable parts of the project was troubleshooting configuration problems.

I encountered several issues while configuring Suricata.

These included:
	*	Incorrect network interface configuration.
	*	Problems with the HOME_NET configuration.
	*	YAML syntax errors.
	*	Problems locating the Suricata rules file.
	*	Rule-file naming/path issues.
	*	Duplicate signature problems.
	*	Understanding why Suricata would not load a configuration successfully.

For example, I encountered an error similar to:

failed to parse line 20 expected ':'

This taught me that Suricata’s YAML configuration is sensitive to formatting and syntax.

I also encountered an issue where Suricata could not find the expected rule file:

no rule file match /etc/suricata/rules/suricata.rules

This required checking the rule path and configuration.

Suricata Rule Loading

The project also taught me that creating a rule is not enough. The rule must actually be loaded by the Suricata engine. The configuration referenced the rules directory:

default-rule-path: /etc/suricata/rules and the rule file.

This distinction was important:

Rule created
     ↓
Rule correctly located
     ↓
Rule referenced by configuration
     ↓
Suricata successfully loads rule
     ↓
Traffic matches rule
     ↓
Alert generated

Starting Suricata

Suricata was configured to run against the monitoring interface. The general Suricata command used in the lab was: sudo suricata -c /etc/suricata/suricata.yaml -i enp0s8

Where: -c specifies the configuration file. And: -i enp0s8 specifies the network interface Suricata should monitor.

